{"authored" [{"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=2t,\\,\\,y+3={t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=4t,\\,\\,y+3=2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=2t,\\,\\,y-3={t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=4{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+5)^{2}=-2(y+2)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+5)^{2}=-8(y+2)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-5)^{2}=4(y-2)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-5)^{2}=8(y-2)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4x+12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac26007af0de8d73ef46c33d4c1b9bd3a7b690eb63c7f555a4a2f76733ffb76a" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7446.json" "file_sha256" "b8167f286cf4ecf1acfed3d9a924e89cbccf680ebb242ba3e464dc67c0762aed" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7446.json" "file_sha256" "b8167f286cf4ecf1acfed3d9a924e89cbccf680ebb242ba3e464dc67c0762aed" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7447.json" "file_sha256" "6289f406bf4c6acdde124e8a328d4f1d6df33a57d646fba820c07a23087aacaa" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7447.json" "file_sha256" "6289f406bf4c6acdde124e8a328d4f1d6df33a57d646fba820c07a23087aacaa" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7448.json" "file_sha256" "fcb937886143d1b1873c731e0916e6a2a352bf9aa846c1b9a6583e0020edb926" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7448.json" "file_sha256" "fcb937886143d1b1873c731e0916e6a2a352bf9aa846c1b9a6583e0020edb926" "basis" 1129 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "at