" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7d1e087ea67f5506f9421a25af5f110dcda30e884c080d653a1a45f5d7c3833" "basis" 1339 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,4)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-1,4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=2pt \\\\ 1=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(1)t,\\,y-3=(1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=-2t,\\,y+3=-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=2t,\\,y+3={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=-2t,\\,y-3=-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=2t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/91500faad34245d5b2389ae71aa39f34b10b9a75b1b75307b5aa6231b311307b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(1,2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-4),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y-2=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=-3t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84821" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=2t,\\,\\,y+3={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+3=4t,\\,\\,y+3=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=2t,\\,\\,y-3={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=4{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t"] ["math" "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t"] ["math" "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t"] ["math" "x+4={t}^{2},\\,y-2=t"] ["math" "x-4=-{t}^{2},\\,y+2=-2t"] ["math" "x-4={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t"] ["math" "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t"] ["math" "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t"] ["math" "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+5=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+3=12t"] ["math" "x+5=-{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t"] ["math" "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t"] ["math" "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=3t"] ["math" "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-12t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t"] ["math" "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t"] ["math" "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4(x-3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y-1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x-4}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-4}{2}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-8(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y+1)^{2}=-8(x+4)"] ["math" "(y+1)^{2}=4(x+4)"] ["math" "(y-1)^{2}=-4(x-4)"] ["math" "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)"] ["math" "(y-1)^{2}=8(x-4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-6}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-6)^{2}=24(y-3)"] ["math" "(x-6)^{2}=6(y-3)"] ["math" "(x-6)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y-3)"] ["math" "(y-3)^{2}=6(x-6)"] ["math" "(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(x-6)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-6)^{2}=6(y-3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+5)^{2}=-2(y+2)"] ["math" "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)"] ["math" "(x+5)^{2}=-8(y+2)"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}=4(y-2)"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}=8(y-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2{t}^{2},\\,y-4=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-4}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+3}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+3}{2} \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+3}{2}) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =8(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7"} {"question" "q-84853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y+4)^{2}=2(x-3)"] ["math" "(y+4)^{2}=8(x-3)"] ["math" "(y-4)^{2}=16(x+3)"] ["math" "(y-4)^{2}=2(x+3)"] ["math" "(y-4)^{2}=8(x+3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "q-84853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(y-4)^{2}=8(x+3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "470ab36b5b9904c56e82e1a178cf07540e621b827fce2402fd62ea836ab5fed7"} {"question" "e-7446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/example-7446.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d959af5495d583156b4e9f5c2785751655252f33c14ccdd7c8f9634012284"} {"question" "e-7446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/example-7446.json" "file_sha256" "dc1d959af5495d583156b4e9f5c2785751655252f33c14ccdd7c8f9634012284"} {"question" "e-7447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/example-7447.json" "file_sha256" "324d5cbc035512075b1e3dc326897457b1ca457393d25b039b80e08bec082e97"} {"question" "e-7447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/example-7447.json" "file_sha256" "324d5cbc035512075b1e3dc326897457b1ca457393d25b039b80e08bec082e97"} {"question" "e-7448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/example-7448.json" "file_sha256" "fcb937886143d1b1873c731e0916e6a2a352bf9aa846c1b9a6583e0020edb926"} {"question" "e-7448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/example-7448.json" "file_sha256" "fcb937886143d1b1873c731e0916e6a2a352bf9aa846c1b9a6583e0020edb926"}] "usage" {"q-84469" [] "q-106447" [] "q-323229" [] "q-84843" [] "e-7446" [] "e-7447" [] "q-106455" [] "q-84854" [] "q-84840" [] "e-7448" []} "basis" 1478 "retractions" [[17592186288353 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?"] [17592186301503 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?"] [17592186301496 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?"] [17592186157785 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x - 6 = 3t, y - 3 = \\frac{3}{2}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."] [17592186157040 :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/1a75a5242f363